Relevant columns and why we need them  
• event_date STRING : primary calendar field; will be parsed to DATE for cohort and retention‑week boundaries; condition on 20180709‑20181002.  
• _TABLE_SUFFIX (pseudo) : comes from wildcard tables; same value as event_date so we can restrict the scan to the required days.  
• event_name STRING : filter to only the “session_start” events (first session & later activity).  
• user_pseudo_id STRING : anonymous user id that lets us follow a person across days.  
• user_first_touch_timestamp INT64 : in µs; when its date part equals event_date the row represents the very first visit, so the user belongs to the “new‑user” cohort.  

Typical conditions we will apply  
• WHERE event_name = 'session_start'  
• WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20180709' AND '20181002'  
• WHERE event_date = FORMAT_TIMESTAMP('%Y%m%d', TIMESTAMP_MICROS(user_first_touch_timestamp))   -- “new user” check  
• Cohort weeks start on Monday: DATE_TRUNC(PARSE_DATE('%Y%m%d', event_date), WEEK(MONDAY))  

Below are ten exploratory → final SQL snippets (BigQuery StandardSQL).  
All queries return at most 20 rows to stay light.

